WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.000
This project builds an explainable state estimator for a mobile robot by combining noisy position and velocity readings.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Success means lower position error, deterministic behavior, valid covariance, and safe handling of invalid inputs.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:36.000
The toolchain uses Jupyter, Python, NumPy, unit tests, and a future ROS 2 integration boundary.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:48.000
The state contains planar position and velocity, while a four-by-four covariance matrix represents uncertainty.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Prediction advances position using velocity and elapsed time, while process noise models imperfect motion.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:12.000
At 1.2 meters per second over 0.1 seconds, the predicted x position advances by 0.12 meters.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:24.000
The G matrix maps acceleration uncertainty into position and velocity uncertainty.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Innovation measures the difference between measurement and prediction; the Kalman gain determines the correction weight.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:48.000
With predicted variance 0.80 and measurement variance 0.64, the gain is 0.5556.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:02:00.000
A 0.30-meter measurement and 0.12-meter prediction produce a corrected position of 0.22 meters.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:12.000
The implementation keeps the transition matrix and process-noise calculation visible for review.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:24.000
A linear solve, Joseph-form covariance update, and symmetry guard improve numerical stability.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:36.000
The deterministic experiment runs 120 steps with position at 10 hertz, velocity at 5 hertz, and seed 7.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:48.000
The 12-second simulation uses 0.80-meter position noise and 0.18-meter-per-second velocity noise.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:03:00.000
The filtered trajectory stays closer to truth than the raw position measurements.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Position RMSE falls from 1.0101 to 0.2829, a measured improvement of 71.99 percent.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Three deterministic tests verify correction behavior, covariance symmetry, and improvement over raw sensing.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:36.000
The next ROS 2 adapters will ingest timestamped sensor messages and publish pose with covariance.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Future work covers delayed messages, outliers, frame errors, IMU bias, consistency, and real-time C++ benchmarking.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:04:00.000
The core demonstrates transparent mathematics, asynchronous fusion, numerical safeguards, and measurable improvement.
